#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""wq — Lumen autonomous work queue. Atomic claim + lease/visibility-timeout + add-time dedup
+ ENFORCED subsystem locking (slock integration) + enforced claim gates.
Collision-safe across parallel agents at BOTH ticket AND subsystem level: a claim acquires the
ticket lease AND slocks on every declared subsystem in ONE transaction; if any subsystem is held
by another holder, that ticket is skipped and the next eligible one is tried. Prose 'GATED:' notes
are replaced by an enforced `gate` precondition (path[:exists] checks) evaluated before claim.
Lease default 240 min; expired leases auto-revert (SQS visibility-timeout); heartbeat extends
lease AND held slocks. Canonical subsystem vocabulary (2026-07-17): vault-ha, litellm, minerva-db,
queue-db, messenger, research-index, fleet-ssh, cloudflared, fleet-state, wq-tooling.

wq#502 SESSION IDENTITY (2026-08-13): holder identity is no longer free text. Agents register
once per session (`wq whoami --register <base>`) which mints <base>-<6hex> and is exported as
WQ_AGENT. Two sessions sharing a base name used to make every safety mechanism INVERT (subs_free
saw its own name and returned FREE; release_subs let either session drop the other's lock). Now:
registered sessions are distinct strings, a claim is REFUSED when another live session holds the
same base name, and heartbeat/done/release/block refuse when the caller is not the claim holder.
Bare (unregistered) names still work but warn — set WQ_STRICT=1 to make legacy callers refuse.

wq#530 SESSION LIVENESS (2026-08-14): registration alone never expired, so a stopped agent
parked its base name for the full 240m lease TTL and a session that died in its first minute
parked its TICKET for the same 240m. Three separate mechanisms, deliberately NOT one:
  STALENESS (WQ_STALE_MIN, default 45) frees a BASE NAME only. A row is stale when it is idle
    past the threshold AND holds no live lease/slock AND no task is claimed under its bare base
    name. Holding anything live means never stale — a quiet agent mid-experiment is not a dead
    one, and treating it as dead is what released wq#542 out from under a live session.
  PROVISIONAL LEASE (WQ_PROVISIONAL_MIN, default 60) frees a TICKET. A new claim leases for the
    provisional window, and ANY subsequent wq call by the holder promotes it to the full ttl
    (stored per-claim in tasks.lease_ttl). Time alone never releases a claim from an agent that
    is still talking to wq. Reclaim of an expired provisional lease is LOUD and names the holder.
  DEREGISTRATION (`wq whoami --release`) is the only zero-false-positive signal, so a clean exit
    should always use it. It REFUSES while the session still holds work (--force overrides).
agent_activity is an append-only log of every session-touching call: the thresholds above were
picked from claim->done spans, which are upper bounds on the real inter-call gap, because wq kept
no history to measure the real one. It does now.

wq#568 STOP MEANS STOP (2026-08-14): Frank stops an agent; the harness auto-continues it; it
re-reads dispatch, re-claims and burns money on work nobody asked for. A stop now BITES at the
chokepoint instead of being asked for in prose: EVERY subcommand — including read-only list/show —
checks the caller's session at entry against a persistent retired_sessions table and refuses with
a STOP banner (exit 3) when retired. Retirement atomically reverts the session's claims to open
and drops its slocks (the residue the orchestrator hand-patched three times on 08-14). Signals:
  MANUAL       `wq retire <session_id|base_name> [--reason ...]` — operator/orchestrator.
  AUTO-RESPAWN the zero-false-positive one: `whoami --release` plants a 'deregistered' marker;
               ANY later wq call under that identity IS the auto-continue signature (clean exit,
               harness resumes, env still carries the dead id) and upgrades it to retired on the
               spot. Cannot fire on a live agent — a live agent has not deregistered. Side
               effect, accepted: a HUMAN reusing a shell after --release eats one refusal; the
               banner names the remedy (unset WQ_AGENT / register fresh).
  BUDGETS      WQ_SESSION_MAX_AGE (minutes, default 360 = 6h since first_seen) and
               WQ_SESSION_MAX_TICKETS (default 8 closed claims). Env-tunable, logged, ntfy'd —
               for sessions that never exit cleanly. Deliberately NOT the 45m idle threshold:
               wq#530 measured idle as safe for freeing NAMES and unsafe for freeing WORK.
               A legitimate marathon session raises the env; it does not edit shared tooling.
SCOPE, stated rather than discovered: this gates claim/coordinate/land (wq + slock + lumen-pr's
own lock). It does NOT stop a respawn that only edits local files and never calls wq or slock —
no chokepoint exists on that path (same structural answer wq#546 gave for releases).
Metric Frank asked for: refused calls land in agent_activity as RETIRED-REFUSED:<cmd> /
auto-retire:<kind>; count them to measure how often auto-continue actually happens.

wq#582 CLAIM BY ID (2026-08-14): `wq claim --id <n>` claims a SPECIFIC ticket. EVERY claim-by-lane
guard applies with NONE relaxed — deps unmet, another live claim, [DRAFT], blocked/done, gate
unmet, subsystem held — each REFUSES loudly, and there is deliberately no --force. The lane is
taken from the TICKET, and housekeeping (expired-lease reclaim) runs against that lane first, so
a target whose own lease has expired is reclaimed-then-claimed in one call, loudly. An
out-of-order claim prints a NOTICE naming the lane head it stepped past. ONE eligibility ruling
(_eligible) serves the lane scan, the --id check and the head notice — a second copy of these
checks is how the watchdog's L831 happened, and that path is closed here by construction."""
import sqlite3, sys, os, argparse, datetime, socket, re, secrets, json
DB = os.environ.get("WQ_DB", "/home/lumen/agents/work-queue/queue.db")
DEFAULT_TTL_MIN = 240
def _envint(name, default):
    try:
        v = int(os.environ.get(name, "").strip() or default)
        return v if v > 0 else default
    except ValueError:
        return default
# wq#530: both windows are env-tunable so an operator can react without editing shared tooling.
PROVISIONAL_MIN = _envint("WQ_PROVISIONAL_MIN", 60)
STALE_MIN       = _envint("WQ_STALE_MIN", 45)
ACTIVITY_KEEP   = _envint("WQ_ACTIVITY_KEEP", 5000)
# wq#568 budget backstops (minutes / count). NOT the idle threshold — see docstring.
SESSION_MAX_AGE_MIN = _envint("WQ_SESSION_MAX_AGE", 360)
SESSION_MAX_TICKETS = _envint("WQ_SESSION_MAX_TICKETS", 8)
# wq#568: ntfy path is env-overridable so the probe can point it at a stub and PROVE the
# notification fires, instead of asserting it from the source. Production never sets it.
NTFY_BIN = os.environ.get("WQ_NTFY_BIN", "/home/lumen/bin/ntfy-send")
SESSION_RE = re.compile(r"^(?P<base>.+)-(?P<sid>[0-9a-f]{6})$")
STRICT = os.environ.get("WQ_STRICT", "").strip().lower() in ("1", "true", "yes")
FMT = "%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S"
# wq#661: aging escalation — open unclaimed tickets gain +1 effective priority tier per
# WQ_AGING_DAYS days without a claim, capped at 1 tier. Blocked/gated tickets are excluded:
# they are waiting on an identified condition and should not be escalated.
AGING_DAYS = _envint("WQ_AGING_DAYS", 7)
def effective_pri(pri, status, gate, created_at):
    """wq#661: effective priority = stored pri minus age boost (never mutates stored pri).
    Only open/unclaimed tickets are boosted. Blocked and gated tickets are excluded —
    they are waiting on something identified and should not drift up the queue on their own.
    Boost: +1 tier per AGING_DAYS days open, capped at 1. effective_pri never goes below 1."""
    if status != 'open': return pri
    if gate and gate.strip(): return pri  # gated — has an identified blocking condition
    try:
        created = datetime.datetime.strptime(created_at, FMT).replace(tzinfo=datetime.timezone.utc)
        days = (utcnow() - created).total_seconds() / 86400.0
    except (TypeError, ValueError):
        return pri
    boost = min(1, int(days / AGING_DAYS))
    return max(1, pri - boost)
CANON_SUBSYSTEMS = {"vault-ha","litellm","minerva-db","queue-db","messenger",
                    "research-index","fleet-ssh","cloudflared","fleet-state","wq-tooling",
                    # wq#512 (2026-08-13): cross-lane shared assets. The github lane and the
                    # pokemon lane operate on the SAME repo and the SAME working tree, so the
                    # boundary has to be a real lock, not prose. See LANE_BOUNDARIES.md.
                    "lpp-repo",   # the frankstupak/lumen-plays-pokemon repo AS PUBLISHED:
                                  # branches, tags, releases, rulesets, published history
                    "lpp-tree",   # the PRIMARY working tree /home/lumen/pokemon
                                  # (linked worktrees under scratch/worktrees are NOT this)
                    "ccc-repo",   # frankstupak/claude-command-center + its primary clone
                    "mc-tree"}    # mission-control PRIMARY tree /home/lumen/mission-control
                                  # (app.py + ui/; linked worktrees are NOT this) — mc-v2 2026-08-21
# wq#912: 27 open tickets sat in 7 lanes the dispatcher could never see because their lane
# had no dispatcher/config.json entry — mis-laned at ticket-creation time, never caught until
# an audit went looking. LANE_EXEMPT names the lanes that deliberately have no entry (not a
# mis-lane): 'auto' is wq's own CLI default (wq#830: spans arbitrary repos, never one lane's
# repo); 'user' is Frank-only (wq itself already refuses agent claims from it); 't1' stays
# lumen_dispatch's (DESIGN.md §13: "No t1 lane takeover"). Anything else needs a real entry.
LANE_EXEMPT = {"auto", "user", "t1"}
DISPATCHER_CONFIG = os.environ.get("WQ_DISPATCHER_CONFIG",
                                   "/home/lumen/foreman/dispatcher/config.json")
def configured_lanes():
    """wq#912: lanes with a live dispatcher/config.json entry. None means the config could
    not be read — callers must fail OPEN (a missing/unreadable file never blocks `wq add`)."""
    try:
        with open(DISPATCHER_CONFIG) as f:
            cfg = json.load(f)
        return set(cfg.get("lanes", {}).keys())
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return None
def utcnow(): return datetime.datetime.now(datetime.timezone.utc)
def now(): return utcnow().strftime(FMT)
def lease_from(minutes): return (utcnow() + datetime.timedelta(minutes=minutes)).strftime(FMT)
def norm_title(t): return re.sub(r"[^a-z0-9]+", " ", (t or "").lower()).strip()
def parse_subs(s):
    return [x.strip().lower() for x in (s or "").replace(",", " ").split() if x.strip()]
# wq#617: (model, effort) vocabulary. Full CLI ids only (a bare alias like "sonnet" is what
# the router resolves; the ticket stores what `claude --model` will be handed). Effort names
# only — the CLI takes low|medium|high|xhigh|max, never integers (FOREMAN_RESEARCH §3).
EFFORTS = ("low", "medium", "high", "xhigh", "max")
MODEL_RE = re.compile(r"^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9._-]*$")
def norm_tuple(model, effort):
    model = (model or "").strip() or None
    effort = (effort or "").strip().lower() or None
    if model and not MODEL_RE.match(model):
        print(f"REFUSED: bad model id {model!r}"); sys.exit(2)
    if effort and effort not in EFFORTS:
        print(f"REFUSED: effort must be one of {'|'.join(EFFORTS)} (names, never integers), got {effort!r}"); sys.exit(2)
    if effort and not model:
        print("REFUSED: --effort without --model"); sys.exit(2)
    return model, effort
def conn():
    c = sqlite3.connect(DB, timeout=30)
    c.execute("PRAGMA journal_mode=WAL")
    c.execute("PRAGMA busy_timeout=30000")
    return c
def init():
    c = conn()
    c.execute("""CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS tasks(
        id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT, lane TEXT DEFAULT 'auto',
        pri INTEGER DEFAULT 2, title TEXT, detail TEXT, deps TEXT DEFAULT '',
        status TEXT DEFAULT 'open', claimed_by TEXT, claimed_at TEXT,
        done_at TEXT, notes TEXT DEFAULT '', created_at TEXT)""")
    # slocks table shared with bin/slock (same DB) — ensure present so wq works standalone
    c.execute("""CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS slocks(
        scope TEXT PRIMARY KEY, holder TEXT, acquired_at TEXT,
        expires_at TEXT, note TEXT DEFAULT '')""")
    # wq#502: session identity registry. ADDITIVE ONLY — no column added to tasks/slocks,
    # so in-flight claims and every other reader (queue-status, slock, lumen-sqlutil) are untouched.
    c.execute("""CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS agent_sessions(
        session_id TEXT PRIMARY KEY, base_name TEXT NOT NULL, host TEXT, pid INTEGER,
        first_seen TEXT, last_seen TEXT)""")
    c.execute("CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_agent_sessions_base ON agent_sessions(base_name)")
    # wq#530: append-only activity log. ADDITIVE — no existing table or column changes, so
    # queue-status, mctl, slock and lumen-sqlutil are untouched.
    c.execute("""CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS agent_activity(
        id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT, session_id TEXT, base_name TEXT,
        cmd TEXT, ts TEXT)""")
    c.execute("CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_agent_activity_sid ON agent_activity(session_id,id)")
    cols = {r[1] for r in c.execute("PRAGMA table_info(tasks)")}
    if "lease_expires" not in cols: c.execute("ALTER TABLE tasks ADD COLUMN lease_expires TEXT")
    if "subsystems"   not in cols: c.execute("ALTER TABLE tasks ADD COLUMN subsystems TEXT DEFAULT ''")
    if "gate"         not in cols: c.execute("ALTER TABLE tasks ADD COLUMN gate TEXT DEFAULT ''")
    # wq#530: the ttl the claimer asked for, so promotion restores THAT and never overrides an
    # explicit --ttl with the global default. NULL on every pre-existing row = DEFAULT_TTL_MIN.
    if "lease_ttl"    not in cols: c.execute("ALTER TABLE tasks ADD COLUMN lease_ttl INTEGER")
    # wq#617 (foreman T3): the orchestrator's (model, effort) assignment + the archetype it was
    # derived from, written INTO the ticket so any dispatcher (fworker, agent-prompt, a human)
    # reads the same tuple. ADDITIVE — NULL on every pre-existing row = "unassigned, router
    # decides at dispatch". Values are the CLI strings (claude-sonnet-5 / high), never integers.
    if "model"        not in cols: c.execute("ALTER TABLE tasks ADD COLUMN model TEXT")
    if "effort"       not in cols: c.execute("ALTER TABLE tasks ADD COLUMN effort TEXT")
    if "archetype"    not in cols: c.execute("ALTER TABLE tasks ADD COLUMN archetype TEXT")
    # wq#741 via wq#841(a2): _finish writes these on every done/block. Lost in the 2026-08-23
    # 12:17Z revert of /home/lumen/bin/wq; restored here because a fresh DB is unusable without
    # them. block_reason/blocked_at are cleared on release/done so a re-block starts fresh.
    if "block_reason" not in cols: c.execute("ALTER TABLE tasks ADD COLUMN block_reason TEXT")
    if "blocked_at"   not in cols: c.execute("ALTER TABLE tasks ADD COLUMN blocked_at TEXT")
    # wq#841 (escalated from wq#787's verify-pass of wq#763): rework needs a DURABLE signal.
    # reopened_count is monotonic and lives in its own column, so nothing any later `wq done`/
    # `wq edit` writes can erase it.
    if "reopened_count" not in cols: c.execute("ALTER TABLE tasks ADD COLUMN reopened_count INTEGER DEFAULT 0")
    if "reopened_at"    not in cols: c.execute("ALTER TABLE tasks ADD COLUMN reopened_at TEXT")
    # wq#662: Frank-decision gate protocol. ADDITIVE — NULL on every pre-existing row.
    # gate_type='frank_decision' marks tickets that enter the frank_gated/pending_review cycle.
    # decision_prompt + orch_recommendation are written by `wq decide` (ORCH-only).
    # frank_response + response_ts are written by `wq answer` (Frank/MC).
    # review_verdict + review_reason are written by `wq review` (ORCH sweep).
    if "gate_type"           not in cols: c.execute("ALTER TABLE tasks ADD COLUMN gate_type TEXT")
    if "decision_prompt"     not in cols: c.execute("ALTER TABLE tasks ADD COLUMN decision_prompt TEXT")
    if "orch_recommendation" not in cols: c.execute("ALTER TABLE tasks ADD COLUMN orch_recommendation TEXT")
    if "frank_response"      not in cols: c.execute("ALTER TABLE tasks ADD COLUMN frank_response TEXT")
    if "response_ts"         not in cols: c.execute("ALTER TABLE tasks ADD COLUMN response_ts TEXT")
    if "review_verdict"      not in cols: c.execute("ALTER TABLE tasks ADD COLUMN review_verdict TEXT")
    if "review_reason"       not in cols: c.execute("ALTER TABLE tasks ADD COLUMN review_reason TEXT")
    # wq#568: retirement is a SEPARATE table, not columns on agent_sessions, because it must
    # SURVIVE the registry row: --release DELETES the row, and a purged/deregistered id calling
    # back in is exactly the respawn this exists to stop. ADDITIVE — nothing else reads it.
    c.execute("""CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS retired_sessions(
        session_id TEXT PRIMARY KEY, base_name TEXT, retired_at TEXT,
        kind TEXT, reason TEXT)""")
    c.execute("CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_retired_base ON retired_sessions(base_name)")
    c.commit(); c.close()

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- wq#502 identity layer
def warn(msg): sys.stderr.write(f"WARNING: {msg}\n")

def is_session(c, ident):
    """Authoritative: a string is a session id ONLY if the registry says so. Never guess from
    shape — base names legitimately contain hyphens (claude-web-0812b)."""
    if not ident: return None
    return c.execute("SELECT session_id,base_name FROM agent_sessions WHERE session_id=?",
                     (ident,)).fetchone()

def base_of(c, ident):
    r = is_session(c, ident)
    if r: return r[1]
    m = SESSION_RE.match(ident or "")
    return m.group("base") if m else (ident or "")

def touch_session(c, ident):
    c.execute("UPDATE agent_sessions SET last_seen=? WHERE session_id=?", (now(), ident))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- wq#530 liveness layer
def log_activity(c, ident, cmd):
    """Append-only. Never allowed to break the command that emitted it."""
    try:
        c.execute("INSERT INTO agent_activity(session_id,base_name,cmd,ts) VALUES(?,?,?,?)",
                  (ident, base_of(c, ident), cmd, now()))
        c.execute("DELETE FROM agent_activity WHERE id <= "
                  "(SELECT MAX(id) FROM agent_activity) - ?", (ACTIVITY_KEEP,))
    except sqlite3.Error:
        pass

def idle_minutes(last_seen):
    try:
        t = datetime.datetime.strptime(last_seen, FMT).replace(tzinfo=datetime.timezone.utc)
    except (TypeError, ValueError):
        return None
    return (utcnow() - t).total_seconds() / 60.0

def session_holdings(c, sid, t_now):
    held = [r[0] for r in c.execute(
        """SELECT id FROM tasks WHERE claimed_by=? AND status='claimed'
           AND lease_expires IS NOT NULL AND lease_expires >= ?""", (sid, t_now))]
    locks = [r[0] for r in c.execute(
        "SELECT scope FROM slocks WHERE holder=? AND expires_at >= ?", (sid, t_now))]
    return held, locks

def promote_leases(c, ident):
    """ANY wq call by the holder is evidence of life: restore every provisional lease it holds
    to the ttl that claim asked for, and extend the slocks with it so the two never diverge.
    Only ever LENGTHENS, and never touches a lease that has already expired — that one may
    already belong to someone else."""
    if not ident: return 0
    t_now = now(); n = 0
    for tid, subsystems, exp, ttl in c.execute(
            """SELECT id, subsystems, lease_expires, lease_ttl FROM tasks
               WHERE claimed_by=? AND status='claimed'
                 AND lease_expires IS NOT NULL AND lease_expires >= ?""", (ident, t_now)).fetchall():
        full = int(ttl) if ttl else DEFAULT_TTL_MIN
        target = lease_from(full)
        if target <= exp: continue
        c.execute("UPDATE tasks SET lease_expires=? WHERE id=?", (target, tid))
        subs = parse_subs(subsystems)
        if subs: acquire_subs(c, subs, ident, full, f"wq#{tid}")
        n += 1
    return n

def mark_alive(c, ident, cmd):
    """One call per subcommand. Registered sessions get last_seen + an activity row; ANY holder
    (registered or bare) gets its provisional leases promoted."""
    if not ident: return
    if is_session(c, ident):
        touch_session(c, ident)
        log_activity(c, ident, cmd)
    promote_leases(c, ident)

def ping(cmd):
    """Best-effort liveness ping for read-only subcommands. A liveness signal must never be able
    to fail the command that carries it."""
    ident = os.environ.get("WQ_AGENT", "").strip()
    if not ident: return
    try:
        c = conn(); c.isolation_level = None
        c.execute("BEGIN IMMEDIATE")
        mark_alive(c, ident, cmd)
        c.execute("COMMIT"); c.close()
    except sqlite3.Error:
        pass

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- wq#568 retirement layer
def retired_row(c, ident):
    if not ident: return None
    return c.execute("SELECT session_id, base_name, retired_at, kind, reason "
                     "FROM retired_sessions WHERE session_id=?", (ident,)).fetchone()

def _ntfy(title, msg):
    """Best-effort, bounded. A notification must never be able to fail the command."""
    try:
        import subprocess
        subprocess.run([NTFY_BIN, "-t", "lumen-fleet", "-T", title, msg],
                       capture_output=True, timeout=15)
    except Exception:
        pass

def release_session_holdings(c, sid):
    """Revert every claimed ticket to open and drop every slock for this holder. The atomic
    half of retirement — a respawn cannot land or hold anything, and the orchestrator stops
    hand-editing status to clean claimed_by residue."""
    t_now = now()
    tickets = [r[0] for r in c.execute(
        "SELECT id FROM tasks WHERE claimed_by=? AND status='claimed'", (sid,))]
    if tickets:
        c.execute("""UPDATE tasks SET status='open', claimed_by=NULL, claimed_at=NULL,
                     lease_expires=NULL, lease_ttl=NULL
                     WHERE claimed_by=? AND status='claimed'""", (sid,))
    locks = [r[0] for r in c.execute("SELECT scope FROM slocks WHERE holder=?", (sid,))]
    if locks:
        c.execute("DELETE FROM slocks WHERE holder=?", (sid,))
    return tickets, locks

def mark_retired(c, sid, base, kind, reason):
    c.execute("""INSERT INTO retired_sessions(session_id, base_name, retired_at, kind, reason)
                 VALUES(?,?,?,?,?)
                 ON CONFLICT(session_id) DO UPDATE SET retired_at=excluded.retired_at,
                   kind=excluded.kind, reason=excluded.reason""",
              (sid, base, now(), kind, reason))
    c.execute("DELETE FROM agent_sessions WHERE session_id=?", (sid,))

def stop_banner(sid, retired_at, kind, reason):
    b = ("\n================== STOP — SESSION RETIRED ==================\n"
         f"Session '{sid}' was retired at {retired_at}Z ({kind}):\n"
         f"  {reason}\n"
         "THIS SESSION MUST STOP NOW.\n"
         "Do not claim, do not lock, do not resume work, do not re-register.\n"
         "If you are an auto-continued agent: END YOUR RUN IMMEDIATELY.\n"
         "(A human reusing this shell: unset WQ_AGENT, or register a fresh\n"
         " name in a clean environment. This identity stays dead.)\n"
         "============================================================")
    print(b); sys.stderr.write(b + "\n")

def enforce_retirement(a):
    """wq#568 entry gate — runs before EVERY subcommand, read-only ones included, because a
    respawn's first act is a wq call and this is where a stop can bite. Ordering inside:
      1. already-retired          -> log the refusal (the metric), banner, exit 3.
      2. 'deregistered' marker    -> the auto-continue signature: upgrade to retired, release
                                     anything --force left behind, ntfy, banner, exit 3.
      3. live registered session  -> budget backstops (age / closed tickets); trip = retire,
                                     release, ntfy, banner, exit 3.
      4. unregistered legacy name -> untouched (wq#530 backcompat, pinned by Frank), UNLESS
                                     that exact name was explicitly retired (case 1 catches it).
    The happy path costs one indexed SELECT."""
    ident = getattr(a, "agent", None) or getattr(a, "by", None) \
            or os.environ.get("WQ_AGENT", "").strip()
    if not ident: return
    ident = ident.strip()
    try:
        c = conn()
        r = retired_row(c, ident)
        if r:
            sid, base, rat, kind, reason = r
            if kind == "deregistered":
                c.isolation_level = None
                c.execute("BEGIN IMMEDIATE")
                tk, lk = release_session_holdings(c, ident)
                reason = ("auto-continue signature: wq call after clean deregistration "
                          f"(deregistered {rat}Z, first refused cmd={getattr(a,'cmd','?')})")
                mark_retired(c, ident, base, "auto-respawn", reason)
                log_activity(c, ident, "auto-retire:respawn")
                c.execute("COMMIT"); c.close()
                _ntfy("wq auto-retire (respawn)",
                      f"{ident}: wq '{getattr(a,'cmd','?')}' after clean deregistration — "
                      f"auto-continued agent stopped at the chokepoint."
                      + (f" released tickets={tk}" if tk else "")
                      + (f" dropped slocks={lk}" if lk else ""))
                stop_banner(ident, now(), "auto-respawn", reason)
            else:
                c.isolation_level = None
                c.execute("BEGIN IMMEDIATE")
                log_activity(c, ident, f"RETIRED-REFUSED:{getattr(a,'cmd','?')}")
                c.execute("COMMIT"); c.close()
                stop_banner(ident, rat, kind, reason or "(no reason recorded)")
            sys.exit(3)
        row = is_session(c, ident)
        if row:
            fs = c.execute("SELECT first_seen FROM agent_sessions WHERE session_id=?",
                           (ident,)).fetchone()[0]
            age = idle_minutes(fs)  # minutes since first_seen; None if unparseable
            done_n = c.execute("SELECT COUNT(*) FROM tasks WHERE claimed_by=? AND status='done'",
                               (ident,)).fetchone()[0]
            trip = None
            if age is not None and age > SESSION_MAX_AGE_MIN:
                trip = ("auto-age", f"session age {age:.0f}m exceeds WQ_SESSION_MAX_AGE="
                                    f"{SESSION_MAX_AGE_MIN}m (budget backstop; raise the env "
                                    f"for a legitimate marathon session)")
            elif done_n >= SESSION_MAX_TICKETS:
                trip = ("auto-tickets", f"{done_n} closed claims reaches WQ_SESSION_MAX_TICKETS="
                                        f"{SESSION_MAX_TICKETS} (budget backstop)")
            if trip:
                kind, reason = trip
                c.isolation_level = None
                c.execute("BEGIN IMMEDIATE")
                tk, lk = release_session_holdings(c, ident)
                mark_retired(c, ident, row[1], kind, reason)
                log_activity(c, ident, f"auto-retire:{kind}")
                c.execute("COMMIT"); c.close()
                _ntfy(f"wq auto-retire ({kind})",
                      f"{ident}: {reason}."
                      + (f" released tickets={tk}" if tk else "")
                      + (f" dropped slocks={lk}" if lk else ""))
                stop_banner(ident, now(), kind, reason)
                sys.exit(3)
        c.close()
    except sqlite3.Error:
        # The gate must never brick wq on a broken/locked DB — the underlying command's own
        # error handling owns that. Failing OPEN here only skips the gate for one call.
        pass

def partition_for_base(c, base, exclude_sid):
    """Split every registry row for this base into BLOCKING and STALE.

    A row is STALE only when all three hold: idle past WQ_STALE_MIN, holding no live lease or
    slock, and no task claimed under the literal bare base name. The last clause is not
    paranoia — owner_ok() lets a registered session adopt a claim held under its own bare base
    name, so two sessions sharing a base while such a claim exists is the one wq#502 inversion
    that session-unique ids did NOT close."""
    t_now = now()
    bare = [r[0] for r in c.execute(
        """SELECT id FROM tasks WHERE claimed_by=? AND status='claimed'
           AND lease_expires IS NOT NULL AND lease_expires >= ?""", (base, t_now))]
    blocking, stale = [], []
    for sid, fs, ls in c.execute(
            "SELECT session_id, first_seen, last_seen FROM agent_sessions "
            "WHERE base_name=? AND session_id<>?", (base, exclude_sid or "")).fetchall():
        held, locks = session_holdings(c, sid, t_now)
        idle = idle_minutes(ls)
        row = (sid, held, locks, fs, ls, idle)
        if held or locks or idle is None or idle < STALE_MIN or bare:
            blocking.append(row)
        else:
            stale.append(row)
    return blocking, stale, bare

def fmt_idle(idle):
    return "idle=?" if idle is None else f"idle={idle:.0f}m"

def purge_stale_sessions(c, ttl_min=DEFAULT_TTL_MIN):
    """(3) sessions idle > lease TTL auto-expire — but NEVER purge one still holding a live
    lease or slock, or we would lose the ability to name the blocking holder."""
    cutoff = (utcnow() - datetime.timedelta(minutes=ttl_min)).strftime(FMT)
    t_now = now()
    c.execute("""DELETE FROM agent_sessions WHERE last_seen < ?
                 AND session_id NOT IN (SELECT COALESCE(claimed_by,'') FROM tasks
                       WHERE status='claimed' AND lease_expires IS NOT NULL AND lease_expires >= ?)
                 AND session_id NOT IN (SELECT COALESCE(holder,'') FROM slocks WHERE expires_at >= ?)""",
              (cutoff, t_now, t_now))
    return c.execute("SELECT changes()").fetchone()[0]

def live_sessions_for_base(c, base, exclude_sid):
    """Registered sessions sharing this base name that currently hold an ACTIVE lease or slock.
    Used by cmd_claim — steady-state check, deliberately unchanged by wq#537 so existing
    in-flight claims are never retroactively invalidated."""
    t_now = now()
    out = []
    for sid, in c.execute("SELECT session_id FROM agent_sessions WHERE base_name=? AND session_id<>?",
                          (base, exclude_sid or "")):
        held = c.execute("""SELECT id FROM tasks WHERE claimed_by=? AND status='claimed'
                            AND lease_expires IS NOT NULL AND lease_expires >= ?""", (sid, t_now)).fetchall()
        locks = c.execute("SELECT scope FROM slocks WHERE holder=? AND expires_at >= ?", (sid, t_now)).fetchall()
        if held or locks:
            out.append((sid, [r[0] for r in held], [r[0] for r in locks]))
    return out

def resolve_identity(a, c=None):
    """--agent/positional > WQ_AGENT > hostname:pid (legacy, unregistered)."""
    ident = getattr(a, "agent", None) or getattr(a, "by", None) or os.environ.get("WQ_AGENT", "").strip()
    if ident and ident.strip().isdigit():
        print(f"REFUSED: agent name '{ident}' is numeric — that looks like a TICKET id.\n"
              "  `wq claim` does NOT take a ticket id; it hands you the next eligible ticket.\n"
              "  Correct form: wq claim --lane <lane> <your-agent-name>   (or export WQ_AGENT=...)\n"
              "  Register a session first: wq whoami --register <your-agent-name>")
        sys.exit(1)
    if ident: return ident.strip(), True
    return f"{socket.gethostname()}:{os.getpid()}", False

def owner_ok(c, caller, claimed_by, action, tid):
    """(4) an agent may never heartbeat/close/release another session's claim.

    Asymmetric by design, so (4) enforcement and (5) backcompat can both hold:
      1. caller IS the holder                      -> OK
      2. caller registered, holder = caller's OWN
         bare base name                            -> OK (adopting a pre-registration claim;
                                                     safe because registration already refuses
                                                     when another live session owns that base)
      3. caller registered, holder is someone else -> REFUSE. A registered session always knows
                                                     its identity and has no excuse.
      4. caller UNREGISTERED (bare/fallback)       -> warn + allow, unless WQ_STRICT=1. Only
                                                     callers that cannot prove identity get slack.
    """
    if not claimed_by or caller == claimed_by: return True
    caller_is_session = is_session(c, caller)
    if caller_is_session and base_of(c, caller) == claimed_by and not is_session(c, claimed_by):
        warn(f"wq#{tid} was claimed as bare name '{claimed_by}' before you registered; "
             f"adopting it as {caller}.")
        return True
    if caller_is_session or STRICT:
        print(f"REFUSED: wq#{tid} is held by '{claimed_by}', you are '{caller}'.\n"
              f"  An agent may never {action} another session's claim. If that session is dead,\n"
              f"  let its lease expire (auto-reverts to open) or have the orchestrator intervene.")
        return False
    warn(f"wq#{tid} claimed_by '{claimed_by}' but caller is '{caller}' — caller is an "
         f"unregistered legacy name, so this {action} is ALLOWED for backcompat. "
         f"Register with: wq whoami --register <your-agent-name>")
    return True
def deps_met(c, deps):
    if not deps or not deps.strip(): return True
    for d in [x.strip() for x in deps.split(',') if x.strip()]:
        r = c.execute("SELECT status FROM tasks WHERE id=?", (d,)).fetchone()
        if not r or r[0] != 'done': return False
    return True
def gate_met(gate):
    """Enforced precondition. Format: comma-list of 'path' (must exist) or 'path:exists'/'path:missing'.
    Empty gate = always met. Returns (ok, reason)."""
    if not gate or not gate.strip(): return True, ""
    for clause in [x.strip() for x in gate.split(',') if x.strip()]:
        if ':' in clause:
            path, cond = clause.rsplit(':', 1)
        else:
            path, cond = clause, 'exists'
        path = path.strip(); cond = cond.strip().lower()
        present = os.path.exists(path)
        if cond == 'exists' and not present: return False, f"gate: {path} missing"
        if cond == 'missing' and present:    return False, f"gate: {path} present (must be absent)"
    return True, ""
def subs_free(c, subs, holder, t_now):
    """Which declared subsystems are held by a DIFFERENT holder (blocking). Expired = free."""
    blocked = []
    for s in subs:
        r = c.execute("SELECT holder,expires_at FROM slocks WHERE scope=?", (s,)).fetchone()
        if r and r[1] >= t_now and r[0] != holder:
            blocked.append((s, r[0]))
    return blocked
def acquire_subs(c, subs, holder, ttl_min, note):
    exp = lease_from(ttl_min)
    for s in subs:
        c.execute("""INSERT INTO slocks(scope,holder,acquired_at,expires_at,note)
                     VALUES(?,?,?,?,?)
                     ON CONFLICT(scope) DO UPDATE SET holder=excluded.holder,
                       expires_at=excluded.expires_at, note=excluded.note""",
                  (s, holder, now(), exp, note))
def release_subs(c, subs, holder):
    for s in subs:
        c.execute("DELETE FROM slocks WHERE scope=? AND holder=?", (s, holder))
def cmd_whoami(a):
    c = conn(); c.isolation_level = None
    c.execute("BEGIN IMMEDIATE")
    purged = purge_stale_sessions(c)

    # wq#530 (3): the only zero-false-positive liveness signal is the agent saying so itself.
    if getattr(a, "release", False):
        ident, _ = resolve_identity(a, c)
        if not is_session(c, ident):
            c.execute("COMMIT"); c.close()
            print(f"not registered: {ident} — nothing to release (already deregistered, or a bare name)")
            return
        my_base = base_of(c, ident)
        held, locks = session_holdings(c, ident, now())
        if (held or locks) and not a.force:
            c.execute("COMMIT"); c.close()
            print(f"REFUSED: session {ident} still holds work:")
            print(f"  tickets : {held or 'none'}")
            print(f"  slocks  : {locks or 'none'}")
            print("  Deregistering now would leave that work owned by a name the registry no longer\n"
                  "  knows, which is the state manual releases have been cleaning up all day.\n"
                  "  -> hand it back first:  wq done <id> --notes ...   or   wq release <id>\n"
                  "  -> only if it is genuinely abandoned and you know that: --force")
            sys.exit(1)
        log_activity(c, ident, "deregister")
        c.execute("DELETE FROM agent_sessions WHERE session_id=?", (ident,))
        # wq#568: plant the 'deregistered' marker. This is NOT retirement and fires no alert —
        # clean exits are normal. It is the tripwire: any LATER wq call under this identity is
        # the auto-continue signature and upgrades it to retired at the entry gate.
        c.execute("""INSERT INTO retired_sessions(session_id, base_name, retired_at, kind, reason)
                     VALUES(?,?,?,?,?)
                     ON CONFLICT(session_id) DO UPDATE SET retired_at=excluded.retired_at,
                       kind=excluded.kind, reason=excluded.reason""",
                  (ident, my_base, now(), "deregistered", "clean deregistration"))
        c.execute("COMMIT"); c.close()
        if held or locks:
            warn(f"--force: deregistered {ident} while it still held tickets={held or 'none'} "
                 f"slocks={locks or 'none'} — those are now owned by an unregistered name.")
        print(f"DEREGISTERED session: {ident}")
        print(f"  base name '{my_base}' is free for immediate reuse.")
        return

    if not a.register:
        ident, explicit = resolve_identity(a, c)
        r = c.execute("SELECT session_id,base_name,pid,first_seen,last_seen FROM agent_sessions WHERE session_id=?",
                      (ident,)).fetchone()
        mark_alive(c, ident, "whoami")
        c.execute("COMMIT")
        t_now = now(); rows = []
        for sid, bn, fs, ls in c.execute("""SELECT session_id,base_name,first_seen,last_seen
                                            FROM agent_sessions ORDER BY last_seen DESC"""):
            h, lk = session_holdings(c, sid, t_now)
            rows.append((sid, bn, fs, ls, idle_minutes(ls), h, lk))
        c.close()
        print(f"identity : {ident}" + ("" if explicit else "  (fallback — WQ_AGENT not set)"))
        if r:
            _, base, pid, fs, ls = r
            print(f"status   : REGISTERED session (base {base})")
            print(f"           minted {fs}Z, last_seen {ls}Z, by pid {pid} on this host.")
            print(f"           wq#537: that pid belongs to the one-shot `wq whoami --register`")
            print(f"           process that minted this id — it exits immediately and will NEVER")
            print(f"           match a caller's own pid, including this one. It is provenance,")
            print(f"           not a liveness or identity-match signal.")
        else:
            print("status   : UNREGISTERED legacy name")
            print("           -> register with: wq whoami --register <your-agent-name>")
        if rows:
            print(f"\nactive session registry ({len(rows)}) — STALE past {STALE_MIN}m idle (wq#530):")
            for sid, bn, fs, ls, idle, h, lk in rows:
                if h or lk:
                    mark = f"HOLDING tickets={h or 'none'} slocks={lk or 'none'} — never stale"
                elif idle is not None and idle >= STALE_MIN:
                    mark = "STALE — holds nothing, its base name is reusable"
                else:
                    mark = "live"
                print(f"  {sid:<24} base={bn:<16} {fmt_idle(idle):<11} last={ls}Z  {mark}")
        else:
            print("\nactive session registry: (empty)")
        # wq#568 (5): RETIRED is shown distinctly from STALE — stale frees a name, retired
        # kills an identity. 'deregistered' rows are dormant tripwires, labelled as such.
        c2 = conn()
        ret = c2.execute("""SELECT session_id, base_name, retired_at, kind, reason
                            FROM retired_sessions ORDER BY retired_at DESC LIMIT 10""").fetchall()
        c2.close()
        if ret:
            print(f"\nretired / deregistered identities (last {len(ret)}) — wq#568:")
            for sid, bn, rat, kind, reason in ret:
                label = ("clean exit; tripwire armed" if kind == "deregistered"
                         else f"RETIRED ({kind})")
                print(f"  {sid:<24} base={bn:<16} at={rat}Z  {label}"
                      + (f" — {reason[:60]}" if reason and kind != "deregistered" else ""))
        return
    base = a.register.strip()
    if not base or base.isdigit():
        c.execute("COMMIT"); c.close()
        print("REFUSED: --register needs a non-numeric agent name (e.g. rabbit5, gh1)"); sys.exit(1)
    blockers, stale, bare = partition_for_base(c, base, None)
    if blockers and not a.force:
        c.execute("COMMIT"); c.close()
        print(f"REFUSED: base name '{base}' is already registered by a DIFFERENT session:")
        for sid, held, locks, fs, ls, idle in blockers:
            why = ("holds live work" if (held or locks)
                   else f"claim(s) {bare} are held under the BARE name '{base}'" if bare
                   else f"idle only {fmt_idle(idle)}, under the {STALE_MIN}m stale threshold")
            print(f"  {sid}  tickets={held or 'none'}  slocks={locks or 'none'}  "
                  f"{fmt_idle(idle)}  -> {why}")
            print(f"      first_seen={fs}Z last_seen={ls}Z")
        print("  wq#530: a registration is only stepped over when it is idle past "
              f"{STALE_MIN}m AND holds\n"
              "  no lease/slock AND no claim exists under its bare base name. Holding live work\n"
              "  means NEVER stale — a quiet agent mid-experiment is not a dead one.\n"
              "  Two sessions sharing a base name is exactly the failure wq#502 fixes:\n"
              "  every lock check silently inverts and you can take each other's tickets.\n"
              "  wq#537: this now refuses on EXISTENCE, not just an active lease/slock — the old\n"
              "  check missed the startup window where a second registration lands before the\n"
              "  first session has claimed or locked anything.\n"
              "  -> Pick a DISTINCT agent name, or wait for that session to idle out and be purged\n"
              f"     ({DEFAULT_TTL_MIN}m from its last activity, sooner if it releases everything\n"
              "     and a future purge pass runs).\n"
              "  -> Only if you are certain that session is dead: --force")
        sys.exit(2)
    sid = f"{base}-{secrets.token_hex(3)}"
    while c.execute("SELECT 1 FROM agent_sessions WHERE session_id=?", (sid,)).fetchone():
        sid = f"{base}-{secrets.token_hex(3)}"
    c.execute("""INSERT INTO agent_sessions(session_id,base_name,host,pid,first_seen,last_seen)
                 VALUES(?,?,?,?,?,?)""", (sid, base, socket.gethostname(), os.getpid(), now(), now()))
    c.execute("COMMIT"); c.close()
    if stale:
        # NB: distinct loop names. `sid` is the id just minted three lines above, and rebinding
        # it here printed the STALE row's id as the new session's — caught by probe A2.
        print(f"NOTE: stepped over {len(stale)} STALE registration(s) for base '{base}' (wq#530):")
        for s_sid, _h, _l, _fs, s_ls, s_idle in stale:
            print(f"  {s_sid}  {fmt_idle(s_idle)} (>{STALE_MIN}m), holding nothing, last={s_ls}Z")
        print("  They keep their registry rows so the history stays readable; they just no")
        print("  longer park the name. If one of them is actually alive, say so on the board.")
    if blockers and a.force:
        warn(f"--force used: '{base}' already live as {', '.join(b[0] for b in blockers)}")
    if purged: print(f"(expired {purged} stale session(s) from registry)")
    print(f"REGISTERED session: {sid}")
    print(f"export WQ_AGENT={sid}")
    print("^ put that in your env; claim/heartbeat/done/release/slock will use it automatically.")

def cmd_add(a):
    subs = parse_subs(a.subsystems)
    bad = [s for s in subs if s not in CANON_SUBSYSTEMS]
    if bad and not a.force:
        print(f"REFUSED: non-canonical subsystem(s): {', '.join(bad)}")
        print(f"Canonical set: {', '.join(sorted(CANON_SUBSYSTEMS))}\nUse --force to override (discouraged).")
        sys.exit(1)
    # wq#912: refuse a lane the dispatcher will never see. Skip silently if the config
    # can't be read (configured_lanes() returns None) — a missing file must never block add.
    lanes = configured_lanes()
    if lanes is not None and a.lane not in lanes and a.lane not in LANE_EXEMPT and not a.force:
        print(f"REFUSED: lane {a.lane!r} has no dispatcher/config.json entry — the dispatcher "
              f"will never see this ticket (wq#912).")
        print(f"Configured lanes: {', '.join(sorted(lanes))}\n"
              f"Exempt (deliberately unlisted): {', '.join(sorted(LANE_EXEMPT))}\n"
              f"Use an existing lane, add one to dispatcher/config.json first, or --force to override.")
        sys.exit(1)
    c = conn()
    if not a.force:
        nt = norm_title(a.title)
        for rid, rtitle, rstatus, rlane in c.execute(
                "SELECT id,title,status,lane FROM tasks WHERE status!='done'").fetchall():
            if norm_title(rtitle) == nt:
                print(f"DUPLICATE — task {rid} [{rlane}/{rstatus}] already has this title:\n  {rtitle}\nUse --force to add anyway.")
                c.close(); sys.exit(1)
    model, effort = norm_tuple(a.model, a.effort)
    # wq#662: --frank-gated implies gate_type=frank_decision and status=frank_gated
    frank_gated = getattr(a, "frank_gated", False)
    gate_type = getattr(a, "gate_type", None) or ("frank_decision" if frank_gated else None)
    init_status = "frank_gated" if frank_gated else "open"
    c.execute("INSERT INTO tasks(lane,pri,title,detail,deps,subsystems,gate,created_at,model,effort,archetype,status,gate_type) VALUES(?,?,?,?,?,?,?,?,?,?,?,?,?)",
              (a.lane, a.pri, a.title, a.detail or '', a.deps or '', ",".join(subs), a.gate or '', now(),
               model, effort, (a.archetype or None), init_status, gate_type))
    c.commit(); print(f"added task {c.execute('SELECT last_insert_rowid()').fetchone()[0]} [{a.lane}]"
                      + (f" status={init_status}" if frank_gated else "")
                      + (f" subsystems={','.join(subs)}" if subs else "")
                      + (f" gate={a.gate}" if a.gate else "")
                      + (f" model={model}@{effort or '-'}" if model else "")); c.close()
def cmd_claim(a):
    if a.lane == 'user':
        print("REFUSED: 'user' lane is Frank-only — never agent-claimable"); sys.exit(1)
    agent, explicit = resolve_identity(a)
    c = conn(); c.isolation_level = None
    c.execute("BEGIN IMMEDIATE")
    t_now = now()
    # wq#502 (2)(3): expire idle sessions, then REFUSE if this base name is live elsewhere.
    purge_stale_sessions(c)
    me = is_session(c, agent)
    if me:
        mark_alive(c, agent, "claim")
    else:
        warn(f"agent '{agent}' is an UNREGISTERED name. Two sessions sharing it will silently "
             f"take each other's tickets. Register once: wq whoami --register <name>")
    my_base = base_of(c, agent)
    blockers = live_sessions_for_base(c, my_base, agent if me else None)
    if blockers:
        c.execute("COMMIT"); c.close()
        print(f"REFUSED: base name '{my_base}' is already held by a different live session:")
        for sid, held, locks in blockers:
            print(f"  {sid}  tickets={held or 'none'}  slocks={locks or 'none'}")
        print("  Refusing to claim — this is the wq#502 collision guard. Use a DISTINCT agent\n"
              "  name (wq whoami --register <name>) or wait for that session's lease to expire.")
        sys.exit(1)
    # wq#582: claim a SPECIFIC ticket. Resolve it FIRST so the housekeeping below runs against
    # the ticket's OWN lane — that is what reclaims the target itself if its lease has expired.
    lane = a.lane
    target_id = getattr(a, "id", None)
    if target_id is not None:
        trow = c.execute("SELECT id, lane, status FROM tasks WHERE id=?", (target_id,)).fetchone()
        if not trow:
            c.execute("COMMIT"); c.close()
            print(f"REFUSED: no such task {target_id} (wq#582: claim --id takes an existing ticket id)")
            sys.exit(1)
        if trow[1] == 'user':
            c.execute("COMMIT"); c.close()
            print(f"REFUSED: wq#{target_id} is in the 'user' lane — Frank-only, never agent-claimable")
            sys.exit(1)
        if a.lane != 'auto' and a.lane != trow[1]:
            print(f"NOTICE: --lane {a.lane} ignored — wq#{target_id} lives in lane '{trow[1]}' and the ticket's lane governs")
        lane = trow[1]
    # reclaim expired ticket leases AND purge expired slocks (single housekeeping pass)
    # wq#530: a reclaim is never silent again. If a lease expired while its holder was alive
    # but quiet, the ONLY way anyone finds out today is by noticing their ticket moved — that
    # is how wq#542 was taken out from under a live session. Name the holder, every time.
    expired = c.execute("""SELECT id, claimed_by, claimed_at, lease_expires, lease_ttl FROM tasks
                           WHERE lane=? AND status='claimed' AND lease_expires IS NOT NULL
                             AND lease_expires < ?""", (lane, t_now)).fetchall()
    if expired:
        c.execute("""UPDATE tasks SET status='open', claimed_by=NULL, claimed_at=NULL,
                     lease_expires=NULL, lease_ttl=NULL
                     WHERE lane=? AND status='claimed' AND lease_expires IS NOT NULL
                       AND lease_expires < ?""", (lane, t_now))
    reclaim_lines = []
    for tid, holder, cat, exp, ttl in expired:
        held_for = idle_minutes(cat)
        gone = idle_minutes(exp)
        window = int(ttl) if ttl else DEFAULT_TTL_MIN
        never_promoted = window > PROVISIONAL_MIN and (idle_minutes(cat) or 0) < window
        reclaim_lines.append(
            f"RECLAIMED #{tid} from {holder or 'unknown'} — claimed "
            f"{'?' if held_for is None else f'{held_for:.0f}'}m ago, lease expired "
            f"{'?' if gone is None else f'{gone:.0f}'}m ago"
            + (" on the PROVISIONAL window (no wq call ever followed the claim)" if never_promoted else ""))
    reclaimed = len(expired)
    c.execute("DELETE FROM slocks WHERE expires_at < ?", (t_now,))
    rows = c.execute("SELECT id,deps,subsystems,gate,title FROM tasks WHERE lane=? AND status='open' ORDER BY pri ASC, id ASC",(lane,)).fetchall()
    pick = None; picked_subs = []; skips = []; head_notice = None
    def _eligible(rid, deps, subsystems, gate, title):
        """wq#582: ONE eligibility ruling for the lane scan, the --id check and the head
        notice. Returns (ok, why, subs, silent) — silent marks deps-unmet, which the scan has
        always passed over without a skip line. A second copy of these checks is how the
        watchdog's L831 happened; this closure is the only place they live now."""
        # wq#532(d): a [DRAFT] ticket is not work, it is a placeholder for work. `claim` handed
        # them out repeatedly until agents invented a parking convention by hand. Enforced here
        # rather than written down, because written down is what failed.
        if (title or "").lstrip().upper().startswith("[DRAFT]"):
            return False, "[DRAFT] — not claimable until the prefix is removed", [], False
        if not deps_met(c, deps):
            return False, f"deps not met ({deps})", [], True
        ok, why = gate_met(gate)
        if not ok:
            return False, why, [], False
        subs = parse_subs(subsystems)
        blocked = subs_free(c, subs, agent, t_now)
        if blocked:
            return False, f"subsystem {blocked[0][0]} held by {blocked[0][1]}", [], False
        return True, "", subs, False
    if target_id is not None:
        # Fresh read AFTER housekeeping — the reclaim pass above may just have reverted the
        # target's own expired lease to open, and that is the intended path for taking over a
        # dead session's specific ticket: reclaimed LOUDLY above, then claimed here.
        rid, st_now, holder, deps, subsystems, gate, title = c.execute(
            "SELECT id, status, claimed_by, deps, subsystems, gate, title FROM tasks WHERE id=?",
            (target_id,)).fetchone()
        refuse = None; ok_subs = []
        if st_now == 'claimed':
            refuse = f"held by '{holder}' with a live lease — wq#502: never take another session's claim"
        elif st_now == 'blocked':
            refuse = "BLOCKED — resolve it or reopen it first"
        elif st_now == 'done':
            refuse = "already done"
        elif st_now == 'frank_gated':
            refuse = "FRANK_GATED — awaiting decision (wq decide / wq answer / wq review)"
        elif st_now == 'pending_review':
            refuse = "PENDING_REVIEW — awaiting ORCH review of Frank's response (wq review)"
        elif st_now != 'open':
            refuse = f"status={st_now}"
        else:
            ok, why, ok_subs, _silent = _eligible(rid, deps, subsystems, gate, title)
            if not ok:
                refuse = why
        if refuse:
            c.execute("COMMIT"); c.close()
            for line in reclaim_lines: print(line)
            print(f"REFUSED: cannot claim wq#{target_id} by id — {refuse}")
            print("  wq#582: every claim-by-lane guard applies to claim --id, none relaxed, and")
            print("  there is deliberately no --force.")
            sys.exit(1)
        pick = rid; picked_subs = ok_subs
        for h_rid, h_deps, h_ss, h_gate, h_title in rows:
            h_ok, _w, _s, _sil = _eligible(h_rid, h_deps, h_ss, h_gate, h_title)
            if h_ok:
                if h_rid != pick:
                    head_notice = (f"NOTICE: out-of-order claim — lane '{lane}' head is "
                                   f"#{h_rid}; wq#{pick} was claimed BY ID (wq#582), on the record.")
                break
    else:
        for rid, deps, subsystems, gate, title in rows:
            ok, why, subs, silent = _eligible(rid, deps, subsystems, gate, title)
            if not ok:
                if not silent:
                    skips.append(f"#{rid} {why}")
                continue
            pick = rid; picked_subs = subs; break
    if pick is None:
        c.execute("COMMIT"); c.close()
        for line in reclaim_lines: print(line)
        msg = f"NONE — lane '{a.lane}' empty or all remaining blocked"
        if skips: msg += " [skipped: " + "; ".join(skips[:6]) + ("; ..." if len(skips) > 6 else "") + "]"
        print(msg); return
    # wq#530 (2): the FIRST lease is short. Promotion to the full ttl is driven by heartbeat —
    # or by any other wq call the holder makes — never by elapsed time alone, because elapsed
    # time cannot tell a dead session from a working one. A session that dies in its first
    # minute frees its ticket in PROVISIONAL_MIN instead of parking it for the full ttl.
    # wq#594: ONE session id driven from TWO places. Captured BEFORE the claim lands so `pick`
    # is not in its own answer. See cmd_concurrency for why this is a symptom detector and not
    # an identity check — an identity check is not available at this chokepoint at all.
    prior_held = [h for h in session_holdings(c, agent, t_now)[0] if h != pick]
    prov = min(PROVISIONAL_MIN, a.ttl)
    lease = lease_from(prov)
    c.execute("""UPDATE tasks SET status='claimed', claimed_by=?, claimed_at=?, lease_expires=?,
                 lease_ttl=? WHERE id=?""", (agent, t_now, lease, a.ttl, pick))
    if picked_subs:
        acquire_subs(c, picked_subs, agent, prov, f"wq#{pick}")
    c.execute("COMMIT")
    t = c.execute("SELECT id,pri,title,detail,deps,subsystems FROM tasks WHERE id=?", (pick,)).fetchone()
    c.close()
    for line in reclaim_lines: print(line)
    for line in skips:
        # wq#532(d): skipped tickets used to be reported ONLY when the lane came back empty, so a
        # [DRAFT] or a gated ticket was passed over in total silence whenever anything else was
        # claimable. A guard nobody can see is indistinguishable from no guard.
        print(f"(skipped {line})")
    if prov < a.ttl:
        print(f"CLAIMED {t[0]} (pri {t[1]}) by {agent} — PROVISIONAL lease until {lease}Z ({prov}m)")
        print(f"  wq#530: the first lease is deliberately short. ANY further wq call you make "
              f"(heartbeat/list/show/done)")
        print(f"  promotes it to the full {a.ttl}m. Go silent for {prov}m and this ticket returns "
              f"to the queue, loudly.")
        print(f"  Long uninterrupted work with no wq calls: run `wq heartbeat {t[0]}` first.")
    else:
        print(f"CLAIMED {t[0]} (pri {t[1]}) by {agent} — lease until {lease}Z (ttl {a.ttl}m; heartbeat to extend)")
    if picked_subs: print(f"LOCKED subsystems: {', '.join(picked_subs)}")
    if head_notice: print(head_notice)
    if prior_held:
        # NOTE, never REFUSE — decided from the data, not from taste. Across the whole
        # registered-session era exactly ONE session ever closed a ticket entirely inside
        # another of its own ticket windows (fleet6-a13cac, wq#565 inside wq#563 — the
        # incident). But it is not the only co-hold: rabbit17b-6f8be9 took wq#522 and wq#547
        # 36s apart and closed both in the same second, which is a deliberate pair. Refusing
        # would have broken that, and a guard that breaks legitimate work gets routed around.
        print(f"NOTICE (wq#594): you ALREADY HOLD "
              f"{', '.join('#' + str(h) for h in prior_held)} on a live lease.")
        print(f"  Normal if you meant to work several at once. If you did NOT claim "
              f"{'that' if len(prior_held) == 1 else 'those'}, a SECOND instance is driving")
        print(f"  '{agent}' — a continuation that inherited WQ_AGENT without re-registering. "
              f"Two drivers under")
        print(f"  one id share /tmp paths, branch bases and worktrees; atomic claims will not "
              f"save those.")
        print(f"  Check: wq concurrency   |   Fix: unset WQ_AGENT; wq whoami --register <name>")
    # wq#598: point at the session's own scratch dir, ONCE, on the first claim of the session.
    # Self-limiting on purpose — the directory's own existence is the "already told you" flag,
    # so no DB query and no per-claim nagging. wq#594 established that a notice printed on every
    # claim is noise rather than signal, and this is guidance, not a finding.
    try:
        _scr = os.path.join(SCRATCH_ROOT, agent.replace("/", "_"))
        if not os.path.isdir(_scr):
            os.makedirs(_scr, mode=0o700, exist_ok=True)
            print(f"SCRATCH (wq#598): write ad-hoc files under {_scr}/ , not bare /tmp.")
            print(f"  /tmp is one flat namespace shared by every live session (5 at once here, "
                  f"most recently)")
            print(f"  and it records nobody's name, so a clobbered file looks exactly like your "
                  f"own. Path: wq scratch")
    except OSError:
        pass  # scratch is a convenience; never let it fail a claim
    print(f"TITLE: {t[2]}\nDEPS: {t[4] or 'none'}\n---\n{t[3]}")
SCRATCH_ROOT = os.environ.get("WQ_SCRATCH_ROOT", "/tmp")

def cmd_scratch(a):
    """wq#598: a per-session scratch directory, so ad-hoc files stop sharing a namespace.

    MEASURED FIRST, and the measurement changed what this is. lumen-pr writes NOTHING to /tmp,
    and no dispatch document contains a /tmp path — so there was no code owner to fix and no
    convention to correct. Every colliding file in /tmp was typed by hand by an agent. Of the
    lumen-owned entries, ~180 carry names any agent would independently pick (a.txt, g.txt,
    o.txt, v1.txt, red.txt, pre.txt, land.txt, chk.txt), and up to 5 sessions run at once here.

    SO BE HONEST ABOUT WHAT THIS IS: guidance, not enforcement. Nothing can stop an agent
    typing `> /tmp/a.txt`. What this does is make the correct path one token long and put it in
    front of the agent at claim time, which is the only moment it is certainly reading.

    The SECOND effect is the one that may matter more than collision-avoidance: /tmp today is
    unattributable. Nothing records who wrote a file, which is why the wq#594 incident was only
    ever caught by a human noticing a PR body they had not written. A path under the session id
    names its writer for free.

    DELIBERATE HOLE, same as the lumen-pr worktree guard: two drivers sharing one WQ_AGENT get
    the SAME directory and still collide. That is correct — it is what wq#594's notice is for.
    """
    ident, explicit = resolve_identity(a)
    d = os.path.join(SCRATCH_ROOT, ident.replace("/", "_"))
    if not a.path_only:
        try:
            os.makedirs(d, mode=0o700, exist_ok=True)
        except OSError as e:
            print(f"could not create {d}: {e}", file=sys.stderr); sys.exit(1)
    print(d)
    if not explicit:
        print("NOTE: WQ_AGENT is not set, so this path is a hostname:pid fallback that CHANGES "
              "every call.\n  Register first: wq whoami --register <name>", file=sys.stderr)

def cmd_concurrency(a):
    """wq#594: report ONE session id being driven from TWO places.

    WHY THIS IS A SYMPTOM DETECTOR AND NOT AN IDENTITY CHECK, stated here because the next
    person will reach for the identity check first, as the filing ticket did:

    There is no per-instance signal available at this chokepoint. Every wq call arrives as a
    freshly-spawned process off ONE shared long-lived gateway, so pid/ppid/sid/pgid are
    per-CALL and boot_id is per-HOST — none of them names a driver. A nonce minted at first
    call fails differently and worse: it would have to live in the environment or a file, and
    a continuation inherits WQ_AGENT through exactly that channel, so it would inherit the
    nonce meant to disambiguate it. Credential-style distinction is not merely unbuilt here,
    it is unavailable. What IS observable is the footprint two drivers leave: concurrent
    holdings under one id. That is what this reports.

    LIMIT, stated rather than discovered later: two drivers that never hold tickets at the
    same time are invisible to this, and so is a driver that only edits files and never calls
    wq — the same structural gap wq#568 recorded for its own scope. This narrows the blind
    spot; it does not close it.

    Exit 1 on a finding so a systemd OnFailure= can fire (wq#565 B5: never pipe it).
    """
    c = conn()
    t_now = now()
    findings = []
    live = c.execute("""SELECT claimed_by, id FROM tasks WHERE status='claimed'
                        AND claimed_by IS NOT NULL AND lease_expires IS NOT NULL
                        AND lease_expires >= ? ORDER BY claimed_by, id""", (t_now,)).fetchall()
    held = {}
    for by, tid in live:
        held.setdefault(by, []).append(tid)
    for by, tids in sorted(held.items()):
        if len(tids) > 1:
            findings.append(("CO-HELD NOW", by,
                             f"holds {', '.join('#' + str(t) for t in tids)} on live leases "
                             f"at the same time"))
    # Historical shape, kept separate: a ticket claimed AND closed entirely inside another of
    # the same session's ticket windows. Strictly rarer than co-holding and the sharper signal
    # — measured across every registered session since wq#502, it matches the wq#594 incident
    # and nothing else.
    rows = c.execute("""SELECT id, claimed_by, claimed_at, done_at FROM tasks
                        WHERE claimed_by IS NOT NULL AND claimed_at IS NOT NULL
                          AND done_at IS NOT NULL""").fetchall()
    per = {}
    for tid, by, ca, da in rows:
        per.setdefault(by, []).append((tid, ca, da))
    for by, items in sorted(per.items()):
        if not SESSION_RE.match(by or ""):
            continue  # bare pre-wq#502 names shared a base by design; not evidence of anything
        for outer in items:
            for inner in items:
                if outer[0] == inner[0]:
                    continue
                if outer[1] < inner[1] and inner[2] < outer[2]:
                    findings.append(("NESTED CLOSE", by,
                                     f"#{inner[0]} [{inner[1]} .. {inner[2]}] closed entirely "
                                     f"inside #{outer[0]} [{outer[1]} .. {outer[2]}]"))
    c.close()
    if not findings:
        print("wq concurrency: no session id shows signs of being driven from two places.")
        return
    print(f"wq concurrency: {len(findings)} finding(s) — one session id, possibly two drivers.\n")
    for kind, by, detail in findings:
        print(f"  [{kind}] {by}")
        print(f"      {detail}")
    print("\nCO-HELD NOW is often legitimate: an agent deliberately working a pair trips it too.")
    print("NESTED CLOSE is the sharper one — a session that finished a whole ticket inside")
    print("another of its own ticket windows was almost certainly two instances, not one.")
    print("If a session did not mean to hold these: unset WQ_AGENT; wq whoami --register <name>.")
    sys.exit(1)

def cmd_heartbeat(a):
    c = conn(); c.isolation_level = None
    c.execute("BEGIN IMMEDIATE")
    r = c.execute("SELECT status, claimed_by, subsystems FROM tasks WHERE id=?", (a.id,)).fetchone()
    if not r or r[0] != 'claimed':
        c.execute("COMMIT"); c.close()
        print(f"REFUSED: task {a.id} is not claimed (status={r[0] if r else 'missing'})"); sys.exit(1)
    caller, _ = resolve_identity(a)
    if not owner_ok(c, caller, r[1], "heartbeat", a.id):
        c.execute("COMMIT"); c.close(); sys.exit(1)
    mark_alive(c, caller, "heartbeat")
    lease = lease_from(a.ttl)
    c.execute("UPDATE tasks SET lease_expires=?, lease_ttl=? WHERE id=?", (lease, a.ttl, a.id))
    subs = parse_subs(r[2])
    if subs: acquire_subs(c, subs, r[1], a.ttl, f"wq#{a.id}")  # extends held slocks for same holder
    c.execute("COMMIT"); c.close()
    print(f"heartbeat {a.id}: lease + {len(subs)} slock(s) extended to {lease}Z (holder {r[1]})")
# ---------------------------------------------------------------- wq#759 evidence gate
# PROBLEM (FLEET_OPS_AUDIT_2026-08-21 F8): 29 done tickets carry no report notes — nothing
# gated closure on evidence, so 'done' meant 'an agent said done'. From here, `wq done`
# refuses unless --notes names something checkable (an existing file path, a PR/commit URL,
# or a commit sha) or the caller states --no-artifact "<reason>" explicitly, on the record.
ARTIFACT_URL_RE = re.compile(r"https?://\S*/(?:pulls?|commits?)/\S+")
# a git sha is hex and 7-40 chars; require >=1 a-f letter so plain decimal tokens (dates,
# ticket ids) never false-positive as shas.
ARTIFACT_SHA_RE = re.compile(r"(?=[0-9a-f]{7,40}$)[0-9a-f]*[a-f][0-9a-f]*")

def _artifact_path_candidates(token):
    """Resolve a bare token to the paths it could mean: as given, relative to the caller's
    cwd (the common case — agents run `wq done` from inside their own worktree), and
    relative to /home/lumen (the fleet home, for notes written from elsewhere)."""
    if os.path.isabs(token):
        return [token]
    return [token, os.path.join(os.getcwd(), token), os.path.join("/home/lumen", token)]

def find_artifact(notes):
    """Does `notes` name a verifiable artifact? Returns (ok, kind, detail)."""
    notes = notes or ""
    m = ARTIFACT_URL_RE.search(notes)
    if m:
        return True, "url", m.group(0).rstrip(".,)")
    for raw in notes.split():
        tok = raw.strip(".,;:()[]\"'")
        if tok and ARTIFACT_SHA_RE.fullmatch(tok):
            return True, "sha", tok
    for raw in notes.split():
        tok = raw.strip(".,;:()[]\"'")
        if not tok or tok.startswith("http"):
            continue
        if "/" not in tok and not re.search(r"\.[A-Za-z0-9]{1,5}$", tok):
            continue  # doesn't look path-shaped — skip rather than stat() every word
        for cand in _artifact_path_candidates(tok):
            if os.path.exists(cand):
                return True, "path", cand
    return False, None, None

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- wq#899 merge-state gate
# PROBLEM (FLEET_OPS_AUDIT_2026-08-23): wq#759 SHAPE-checks the artifact — a PR URL counts as
# evidence the moment it parses. The 2026-08-23 audit found ~30 tickets closed `done` citing a
# PR that was still open, and one of them (wq#825) had its only copy of the code in a working
# tree, where the next clean checkout would have erased it. A PR URL proves a branch exists.
# It does not prove the work landed.
#
# So when the artifact IS a pull-request URL, resolve it. Merged is evidence. Head already
# reachable from the base branch is evidence too — a squash or cherry-pick leaves the PR open
# while the code is genuinely on main. An open PR whose head is nowhere near main is not
# evidence, and `done` refuses it.
#
# FAIL OPEN ON INFRASTRUCTURE, FAIL CLOSED ON FACTS. No token, no network, unknown forge, HTTP
# error, ambiguous answer -> warn and allow. This gate exists to catch a mistake, not to wedge
# every lane in the fleet the first time the forge restarts.
PR_URL_RE = re.compile(r"https?://([^/\s]+)/([^/\s]+)/([^/\s]+?)/pulls?/(\d+)")
MERGE_STATE_TIMEOUT = float(os.environ.get("WQ_MERGE_STATE_TIMEOUT", "8"))

def _forge_token(host):
    """Same search order lumen-pr uses. Returned to the caller, never printed."""
    if host in ("github.com", "www.github.com"):
        return os.environ.get("GITHUB_TOKEN", "").strip() or None
    for path in (os.environ.get("GITEA_TOKEN_FILE") or "/home/lumen/.secrets/gitea-lumen.token",):
        try:
            with open(path) as fh:
                tok = fh.read().strip()
                if tok:
                    return tok
        except OSError:
            pass
    try:
        with open("/home/lumen/agent/.env") as fh:
            for line in fh:
                if line.startswith("GITEA_TOKEN="):
                    tok = line.split("=", 1)[1].strip().strip("\"'")
                    if tok:
                        return tok
    except OSError:
        pass
    return None

def _forge_get(host, path, token):
    import json as _json, urllib.request, urllib.error
    if host in ("github.com", "www.github.com"):
        base, auth = "https://api.github.com", f"Bearer {token}"
    else:
        base, auth = f"http://{host}/api/v1", f"token {token}"
    req = urllib.request.Request(base + path, headers={
        "Authorization": auth, "Accept": "application/json"})
    try:
        with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=MERGE_STATE_TIMEOUT) as r:
            return _json.load(r)
    except (urllib.error.URLError, OSError, ValueError) as e:
        return {"_error": str(e)[:120]}

def _head_on_main(sha, base_ref):
    """Is `sha` already an ancestor of the base branch in the repo the caller is standing in?

    Agents run `wq done` from inside the worktree they did the work in, so cwd is nearly always
    the right clone. Cheap, offline, and it catches the squash-merge case the forge reports as
    'still open'. Any doubt at all -> None, meaning 'no local opinion', not 'no'."""
    import subprocess
    def g(*args):
        return subprocess.run(["git", *args], capture_output=True, text=True,
                              timeout=MERGE_STATE_TIMEOUT)
    try:
        if g("rev-parse", "--git-dir").returncode != 0:
            return None
        if g("cat-file", "-e", sha + "^{commit}").returncode != 0:
            return None
        for ref in (f"origin/{base_ref}", base_ref, "origin/main", "main"):
            if g("rev-parse", "--verify", "--quiet", ref).returncode == 0:
                return g("merge-base", "--is-ancestor", sha, ref).returncode == 0
    except (OSError, subprocess.SubprocessError):
        return None
    return None

def pr_merge_state(url):
    """Resolve a PR URL to (verdict, detail). verdict is one of:
        'merged'  — the forge says it merged
        'landed'  — still open, but its head is already an ancestor of the base branch
        'open'    — open, and the head is not on the base branch: the wq#899 failure
        'unknown' — could not tell (no token, network down, HTTP error, not a PR URL)"""
    m = PR_URL_RE.search(url or "")
    if not m:
        return "unknown", "not a pull-request URL"
    host, owner, repo, num = m.group(1), m.group(2), m.group(3), m.group(4)
    token = _forge_token(host)
    if not token:
        return "unknown", f"no forge token for {host}"
    pr = _forge_get(host, f"/repos/{owner}/{repo}/pulls/{num}", token)
    if "_error" in pr or not isinstance(pr, dict) or "state" not in pr:
        return "unknown", f"{host} unreachable or refused: {pr.get('_error', 'bad response')}"
    if pr.get("merged"):
        return "merged", f"{owner}/{repo}#{num} merged"
    head = (pr.get("head") or {}).get("sha") or ""
    base_ref = ((pr.get("base") or {}).get("ref")) or "main"
    if head and _head_on_main(head, base_ref) is True:
        return "landed", f"{owner}/{repo}#{num} open, but {head[:10]} is already on {base_ref}"
    if pr.get("state") == "closed":
        return "open", f"{owner}/{repo}#{num} was CLOSED without merging"
    return "open", f"{owner}/{repo}#{num} is still OPEN and {head[:10] or 'its head'} is not on {base_ref}"

# wq#841: the single definition of "this ticket came back from the dead". Rework is a move INTO
# an in-flight state FROM a terminal one. A lease reap or a release of a claim nobody finished is
# claimed->open — not rework, and it must not inflate the metric.
TERMINAL_STATUSES = ("done", "rejected")
INFLIGHT_STATUSES = ("open", "claimed", "blocked")
def is_reopen(old_status, new_status):
    return (old_status or "") in TERMINAL_STATUSES and (new_status or "") in INFLIGHT_STATUSES
def bump_reopened(c, tid):
    """Monotonic +1 in its own column. COALESCE covers rows that predate the migration."""
    c.execute("UPDATE tasks SET reopened_count=COALESCE(reopened_count,0)+1, reopened_at=? WHERE id=?",
              (now(), tid))
    return c.execute("SELECT reopened_count FROM tasks WHERE id=?", (tid,)).fetchone()[0]

def _finish(a, new_status):
    """Returns the new reopened_count if this call was a reopen, else 0."""
    c = conn(); c.isolation_level = None
    c.execute("BEGIN IMMEDIATE")
    r = c.execute("SELECT claimed_by, subsystems, status FROM tasks WHERE id=?", (a.id,)).fetchone()
    if r is None:
        c.execute("COMMIT"); c.close(); print(f"REFUSED: no such task {a.id}"); sys.exit(1)
    caller, _ = resolve_identity(a)
    if not owner_ok(c, caller, r[0], new_status if new_status != 'open' else 'release', a.id):
        c.execute("COMMIT"); c.close(); sys.exit(1)
    mark_alive(c, caller, {"done": "done", "open": "release", "blocked": "block"}[new_status])
    if r and r[1]:
        release_subs(c, parse_subs(r[1]), r[0] or "")
    if new_status == 'done':
        c.execute("UPDATE tasks SET status='done', done_at=?, notes=?, lease_expires=NULL, "
                  "lease_ttl=NULL, block_reason=NULL, blocked_at=NULL WHERE id=?",
                  (now(), getattr(a,'notes','') or '', a.id))
    elif new_status == 'open':
        c.execute("UPDATE tasks SET status='open', claimed_by=NULL, claimed_at=NULL, "
                  "lease_expires=NULL, lease_ttl=NULL, block_reason=NULL, blocked_at=NULL "
                  "WHERE id=?", (a.id,))
    elif new_status == 'blocked':
        # wq#741: block_reason/blocked_at are the structural home for this — notes keeps
        # getting the same string too, so `wq show`/`wq list` need no changes.
        c.execute("UPDATE tasks SET status='blocked', notes=?, block_reason=?, blocked_at=?, "
                  "lease_expires=NULL WHERE id=?", (a.reason, a.reason, now(), a.id))
    reopened = bump_reopened(c, a.id) if is_reopen(r[2], new_status) else 0
    c.execute("COMMIT"); c.close()
    return reopened
def cmd_done(a):
    notes = getattr(a, "notes", "") or ""
    no_artifact = getattr(a, "no_artifact", None)
    if no_artifact:
        a.notes = (notes + (" " if notes else "") + f"[NO-ARTIFACT: {no_artifact}]").strip()
    else:
        ok, kind, detail = find_artifact(notes)
        if not ok:
            print(f"REFUSED: wq#759 evidence gate — task {a.id} not closed. `done` requires "
                  f"--notes naming a verifiable artifact (an existing file path, a PR/commit "
                  f"URL, or a commit sha) or an explicit --no-artifact \"<reason>\" recorded "
                  f"on the ticket. Got notes={notes!r}.")
            sys.exit(2)
        # wq#899: the artifact is a PR URL, so the URL is not the end of the check.
        if kind == "url" and "/pull" in detail:
            unmerged = getattr(a, "unmerged", None)
            verdict, why = pr_merge_state(detail)
            if verdict == "open" and not unmerged:
                print(f"REFUSED: wq#899 merge-state gate — task {a.id} not closed. Its evidence "
